      "id": 1,
      "text": "Enigma is a town in Berrien County, Georgia, noted for its unusual name that means puzzle or mystery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enigma%2C_Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was founded between 1876 and 1880 by John A. Ball, who later incorporated it on August 21, 1906.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enigma%2C_Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Ball and his family started a turpentine business around 1878, later shifting focus to lumber as the main industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enigma%2C_Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Enigma sits on the south side of the Brunswick and Western Railroad, with its nearest depot in Brookfield four miles away.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enigma%2C_Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s first postmaster was Ball, who also helped bring Tubb Daughtry and his family to run the local business.",
